WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.520
Speaker 1: Welcome to the David Pakman show.&nbsp;
I'm Farron Cousins and I'm going to be sitting&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:03.520 --> 00:00:08.400
in for David for these next two days. We've&nbsp;
got a lot to cover today. Of course, we've had&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:09.040 --> 00:00:16.320
more from the January six committee.&nbsp;
We've had more from the documentary film&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:16.320 --> 00:00:21.760
that is now a central piece of evidence in those&nbsp;
hearings. We're going to be talking about all&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:21.760 --> 00:00:28.400
that. Plus, Marjorie Taylor Green's week long&nbsp;
meltdown, among many other stories. So let's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:28.400 --> 00:00:34.720
jump right into it. And I want to start by, of&nbsp;
course, talking about the January six hearings&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:35.360 --> 00:00:40.960
and the big takeaway that I have had from&nbsp;
all of these hearings we have seen so far&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:40.960 --> 00:00:45.440
everything that's come out of the committee&nbsp;
and pretty much anything that's been revealed&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:46.080 --> 00:00:55.680
since January 6th of 2021 is that had Donald&nbsp;
Trump had a more competent team working for him,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:56.720 --> 00:01:03.680
I think his coup would have gone a lot differently&nbsp;
and not, of course, in a good way for the United&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:03.680 --> 00:01:10.000
States. So think about this for a moment. One of&nbsp;
the things we learned earlier this week was that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:10.000 --> 00:01:19.200
Mark Meadows was actually actually offering state&nbsp;
officials free MAGA stuff, including autographed&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:19.200 --> 00:01:26.800
MAGA gear. If officials were, you know, willing&nbsp;
to toss out their state's 2020 election results.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:27.920 --> 00:01:34.240
Not exactly a big selling point, right? Yeah.&nbsp;
Like, hey, we know what's unconstitutional. We&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:34.240 --> 00:01:39.680
know you're going to have to break the law, but&nbsp;
we're going to throw in a free hat. How does that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:39.680 --> 00:01:46.800
sound? Right. Nobody in their right mind is going&nbsp;
to take that offer. And then, of course, we have&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:48.160 --> 00:01:53.920
Mike Lindell, who has been spearheading the&nbsp;
efforts to overturn the 2020 election results&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:53.920 --> 00:02:00.080
to this very day. Attorneys like Sidney&nbsp;
Powell and John Eastman and Rudy Giuliani,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:01.120 --> 00:02:08.640
all of this cast of just absolute weirdos.&nbsp;
Working to overturn the 2020 election,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:09.280 --> 00:02:16.480
and all of them, in my opinion, equally&nbsp;
incompetent. And that's the thing, though.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:17.600 --> 00:02:24.400
Had any of these individuals been switched out by&nbsp;
somebody that maybe actually knew what they were&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:24.400 --> 00:02:30.400
doing? You know, somebody with a little bit of a&nbsp;
brain in their heads, Trump could have succeeded,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:31.200 --> 00:02:37.840
you know, switch out. Sidney Powell and&nbsp;
Rudy Giuliani and substitute in Bill Barr.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:39.760 --> 00:02:43.840
Suddenly that becomes a lot scarier if the&nbsp;
attorney general of the United States had gone&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:43.840 --> 00:02:50.080
along with this plan. He would have been able&nbsp;
to use the power of the DOJ. There would have&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:50.080 --> 00:02:56.800
been enough people inside the DOJ to try to carry&nbsp;
out these plans. Think for a second. If instead of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:56.800 --> 00:03:01.920
Mike Lindell going out there and selling this to&nbsp;
the public, which hasn't gone very well for him.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:02.640 --> 00:03:09.760
What if it was Karl Rove instead? Things would&nbsp;
have been a lot different. Rove is a master at&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:09.760 --> 00:03:14.960
messaging. He's an expert manipulator. Whereas,&nbsp;
of course, Mike Lindell just comes across,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:14.960 --> 00:03:20.960
as you know, a bit of an unhinged kind of&nbsp;
guy. He's the pillow man, for God's sakes.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:22.720 --> 00:03:29.280
But you switch out any one of these little weirdos&nbsp;
for somebody with even a little bit of competence.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:29.280 --> 00:03:35.440
A little bit of credibility. Suddenly we're&nbsp;
looking at a very different scenario in the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:35.440 --> 00:03:43.600
United States today. And, of course, therein lies&nbsp;
the bigger problem for the future. Donald Trump,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:43.600 --> 00:03:48.240
of course, and all other Republicans who might&nbsp;
be planning something similar. They've all&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:48.240 --> 00:03:53.920
learned from these mistakes, right? They know the&nbsp;
kind of people that they need to put in place.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:54.720 --> 00:04:00.080
And how do we know that they've learned from this?&nbsp;
Well, you need look no further than Steve Bannon.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:00.080 --> 00:04:05.120
For more than a year now, ever since Trump left&nbsp;
office, really on his podcast, Steve Bannon&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:05.120 --> 00:04:11.200
has been one of the leading cheerleaders telling&nbsp;
these MAGA folks out here across the country,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:11.200 --> 00:04:18.480
go run for office, go become a poll worker.&nbsp;
Run for secretary of state. Run for governor.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:20.400 --> 00:04:28.560
Put yourselves in the position. To where this, you&nbsp;
know, the allegedly stolen 2020 election, which&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:28.560 --> 00:04:34.640
wasn't stolen. But put yourself in the position to&nbsp;
where you can do something about it in the future.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:36.160 --> 00:04:43.920
The framework has been laid down. Okay. It's not&nbsp;
in the planning phases. The framework is there now&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:44.480 --> 00:04:50.960
to a degree. Maybe not to enough of a degree to&nbsp;
actually overturn election results, but they're&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:50.960 --> 00:04:56.720
getting close and there could be a heck of a lot&nbsp;
closer after this year's midterms. And it all goes&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:56.720 --> 00:05:03.920
back to learning from their mistakes. They knew&nbsp;
or they know now. They didn't know at the time,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:03.920 --> 00:05:12.880
but they know now that the people they had running&nbsp;
this whole thing were wholly incompetent. So you&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:12.880 --> 00:05:17.440
replace them with more competent individuals.&nbsp;
You replace them with people who know how to get&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:17.440 --> 00:05:24.320
things done. You replace them with loyal lapdogs&nbsp;
who are not going to tell you no. That, of course,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:24.320 --> 00:05:30.640
has been one of Trump's driving factors with his&nbsp;
endorsements so far in the primary season. That's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:30.640 --> 00:05:38.000
why he tried so desperately to get Brian Kemp and&nbsp;
Brad Raffensperger out of the state of Georgia.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:38.000 --> 00:05:44.400
Because he wants people that would not tell&nbsp;
him no if he were to ask them to break the law.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:46.480 --> 00:05:53.840
And it's already happening. Again, not to not&nbsp;
to the degree that he would like to see it&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:53.840 --> 00:05:57.360
or that other Republicans would&nbsp;
like to see it. But it is happening.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:59.360 --> 00:06:05.840
They know where their weak links&nbsp;
were in 2021, on January 6th.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:06.400 --> 00:06:11.440
And more and more of them, of course, are&nbsp;
being exposed by these January six hearings.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:13.120 --> 00:06:18.560
And they're not going to make the same mistake&nbsp;
twice. And again, that to me is where the real&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:18.560 --> 00:06:22.720
danger lies. It's great that we're exposing&nbsp;
everything that happened that has to be done.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:23.440 --> 00:06:30.480
We also have to hold them criminally accountable&nbsp;
for it. But we can't. You know, once this is over,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:30.480 --> 00:06:38.080
wipe our hands. Cool. Case closed. We've saved&nbsp;
democracy. No. That threat is still out there.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:38.880 --> 00:06:45.600
And that, again, is kind of the underlying theme&nbsp;
of these hearings. It's been the underlying&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:45.600 --> 00:06:51.440
theme also for the record of the judges&nbsp;
overseeing the cases of the Capitol rioters.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:52.880 --> 00:06:58.400
They have said that plenty of these people we've&nbsp;
seen how they've been essentially brainwashed.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:00.560 --> 00:07:04.000
And we believe it can happen again. We&nbsp;
believe these people are still dangerous.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:04.640 --> 00:07:08.800
That's why a lot of the judges overseeing these&nbsp;
cases have been annoyed when prosecutors, you&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:08.800 --> 00:07:12.960
know, reduced the charges down to misdemeanors.&nbsp;
The judges want to put these people in jail.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:14.240 --> 00:07:18.800
Because they're afraid it's going to happen again.&nbsp;
We're getting reports from the Department of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:18.800 --> 00:07:26.000
Homeland Security. You know, to expect violence.&nbsp;
Another report came out yesterday warning of the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:26.000 --> 00:07:31.520
increased risk of violence here in the United&nbsp;
States as we head into the midterms. And all of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:31.520 --> 00:07:40.640
it goes back to January six, folks. Every bit of&nbsp;
it is tied back to that. Because all these people&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:40.640 --> 00:07:47.520
from Trump down to his supporters and everybody&nbsp;
in between, they learned from their mistakes.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:48.640 --> 00:07:51.680
And it's unlikely that they would&nbsp;
be making those same mistakes again.

